    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d779">

                <pb facs="1406" n="1374" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v13p1/pg1374"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.5/4–2354: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Department of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top
                            secret<lb/>niact</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1954-04-23T22:00:00+01:00">April 23, 1954—10
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Dulte 8. No distribution. Eyes only for Acting Secretary from Secretary. Please
                    pass following message to The President: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“I am deeply touched by, and grateful for, your message.<anchor n="1"
                                xml:id="d779-f1"/> The situation here is tragic. France is almost
                            visibly collapsing under our eyes. There is, of course, no military or
                            logical reason why loss of Dien Bien Phu should lead to collapse of
                            French will, in relation both to Indochina and EDC. It seems to me that
                            Dien Bien Phu has become a symbol out of all proportion to its military
                            importance. It does not seem possible to change this situation, although
                            we are not giving up hope and are doing everything possible to keep
                            their spirit up. The position today is a little better as regards EDC,
                            but probably if Dien Bien Phu falls, the government will be taken over
                            by defeatists.</p>
                        <pb facs="1407" n="1375" xml:id="pg1375"/>
                        <p>I shall talk to Eden about Winston’s message and give you my considered
                            judgment shortly. My basic reaction is yours, namely, that a meeting
                            should solemnize and publicize a major decision sufficiently explored
                            and developed in advance to be sure that it is in hand. Faithfully
                            yours, Foster.”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dulles</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
